#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421906} :activity/id #uuid "39b191e6-3a28-55d0-8f36-ad23b66bbcd4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050991 :topic/id #uuid "7789d7fa-bfec-58c3-afd1-afcff30a8748" :topic/math-academy-id 2539} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0adea858-c23c-564c-9506-9e4a60396c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413738} {:answer/id #uuid "22b5f065-43d8-5efe-9322-eca1974cc3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413739} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9614ef-645c-5eb3-83fc-f33c2f35aca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413740} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc4adca-656f-5e68-9890-f48be898ac6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413741} {:answer/id #uuid "f5604891-08a1-55fe-b657-ffce04ef2384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413741} :answer-field/id #uuid "aef76bea-df64-529b-85e7-fa51fb7e165e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "abe7bafd-4069-5c23-9e34-e0cbd57e3732" :question/math-academy-id "q-71465" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x+y+z=0$ and $x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nII. The planes intersect on a line.\n\nIII. The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x+y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\ne ⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} :step/id #uuid "0311a340-7168-5cea-821e-a5c70f05f78d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bbb0f75-5837-5fef-9156-1bb0626a6799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260264} {:answer/id #uuid "358aeb92-2af7-5b63-a3fc-98a16739711a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260355} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae1bb4f-6418-5cbe-8748-f187b44dbec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260517} {:answer/id #uuid "83b18a92-b695-5c83-b721-b2dee329f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261019} {:answer/id #uuid "db99d772-027a-5c17-b647-51f20577ed39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=5,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260517} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e5261fb-a30e-5c21-b272-d448a6432184" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad00dc96-c374-5320-be1d-ca73d7e96f79" :question/math-academy-id "q-129238" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+y-z-3=0$ and $x+2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\nx+y-0-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=3-y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-y)+2y-3(0)+18 & =0 \\\\ 3+y+18 & =0 \\\\ y & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3-y=3+21=24$ and $a=⟨24,-21,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨24,-21,0⟩+t⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=24-21=3,$ and $b=⟨-1,2,1⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "4b62f78b-d432-5341-a2aa-4388a4c716e4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ce110a-db9f-5911-9515-94540ed6dd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336542} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8de119-9259-50a9-9782-4e6da9be6936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336631} {:answer/id #uuid "4c1db453-ad2d-5f60-9a15-9c2d0dec589f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336739} {:answer/id #uuid "e8227368-6969-5434-8958-753fda8ed7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338060} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bff34c-6563-5a40-bee7-7ecfa61e666b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338170} :answer-field/id #uuid "8415a7da-95f3-5c4b-9085-9ccf7a05fb9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "894f2787-9676-52de-a718-3b82f8fd7a1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71463" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x+3y+2z=0$ and $x-y-z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,6,-5⟩.$\n\nII. The planes are parallel.\n\nIII. The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y-z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,3,2⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-6⟩\\ne ⟨1,6,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."} :step/id #uuid "8993a320-0fa1-5bea-8343-47ddcfb4405b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a5b51a0-3005-5ddd-b5ab-b8d3b530fe56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421890} {:answer/id #uuid "77d58298-86f5-5da7-a6f5-5aadeb1af6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=-1,\\,b=⟨1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421891} {:answer/id #uuid "782abb1a-40ca-50f3-9eb2-460b14bea776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421892} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4a733d-fbda-5f24-ba39-11bd9f15b6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421893} {:answer/id #uuid "d67766c9-e639-50ae-a0fa-fe37c24c18e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421896} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee9b9e23-e285-51ba-8f2e-0f93320571fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3582cc5d-9e46-5e0a-8d0c-b0e74f8f5924" :question/math-academy-id "q-71349" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+y-1=0$ and $x+z+1=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+y-1=0\\,⟹\\,y=1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+1=0\\,⟹\\,z=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,1,-1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩+t⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1+(-1)=0,$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "b2250ab1-dcbf-5807-a3a8-ee6a40866bf6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc5a66d-b1b1-585a-b0bc-21d19ca71687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=-18,\\,b=⟨3,9,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421889} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3c1355-587b-53a6-a6d7-2db08ae599af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=-20,\\,b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421894} {:answer/id #uuid "d155552d-65e9-593d-bf4d-41010a2e061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=18,\\,b=⟨3,9,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421895} {:answer/id #uuid "e23fcb96-7207-5675-97d0-1ce03137e6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=20,\\,b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421897} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b80375-370c-5de5-b724-efe10f25c766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=10,\\,b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421898}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421897} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ea7962c-6925-55c2-86b0-52523c308e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "caefbcdd-6450-5b4b-b965-772f5b87b02a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129208" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x+3y-3=0$ and $3x+z+19=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+3y-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+19=0\\,⟹\\,z=-19.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,1,-19⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3y-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,3,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+z+19=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,3,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =3i-2j-9k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,-19⟩+t⟨3,-2,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=1-(-19)=20,$ and $b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "c6d8b30b-6627-55c2-a300-1bd06da939e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421904}}] :activity/title "The Intersection of Two Planes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421888}]}
